{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Expectations vs Reality: TCS Stock Moves on Earnings Surprise",
  "scenario": "You are a junior analyst at Motilal Oswal tracking TCS (Tata Consultancy Services) for a quarterly earnings report. On 13 Oct 2023, TCS reported strong revenue growth of ₹61,854 Cr, but the stock fell 2.3% that day while the broader market rose 1%. Your desk manager asks: Why did good results lead to a price drop? You gather quarterly data, revenue expectations, and price history to diagnose whether the move was an earnings miss, weak guidance, or sentiment.",
  "instructions": "Build a small earnings-vs-expectations analysis sheet: calculate revenue growth rates, compare actual results to consensus expectations, compute the total shareholder return including dividends, and identify which quarters saw gaps between expected and actual numbers.",
  "learning_objective": "Explain why stock prices move on earnings surprises (reality vs expectations), calculate total return including dividends, and diagnose the gap between reported results and market expectations.",
  "hints": [
    "Start by computing quarter-on-quarter and year-on-year revenue growth percentages for each period using simple division.",
    "The Expectation Miss column should show (Actual − Expected) / Expected × 100 — a negative number means the company disappointed the market.",
    "Total Return includes both the price change AND the dividend: (Sell Price − Buy Price + Dividend) / Buy Price × 100."
  ],
